# Security and data handling

Consumption data is personal data under UK GDPR and this service gets audited on it.
Write code here as if a regulator will read the diff, because twice now one has.

## API keys

- `requireApiKey` only checks that `x-api-key` is *present*; the gateway verifies it and this
  service trusts the mesh, so **never expose a meterflow port outside it**. Staging was
  internet-reachable for nine hours after a security-group edit, every route wide open.
- One key per consuming system, never per person, never shared across environments. A key
  that has been on a laptop is a revoked key, and revoking is not the same as rotating.
- Rotation is 90 days with a 14-day overlap: issue, dual-accept, revoke. Do not collapse the
  overlap - the DCC feed retries for 24h, then discards that half-hourly window for good.
- No key material belongs in `Config`. `DATABASE_URL` is the only secret `loadConfig` reads
  and the only one that should ever be added to it. Suspected compromise goes to IT Security.

## MPAN is personal data

- The MPAN *is* the `siteId` throughout, and there is no pseudonymous internal id, so every
  table keyed on `sites(site_id)` - `readings`, `tariffs` - is personal data as well.
- An MPAN plus half-hourly readings is an occupancy record; that pairing sets the clock below.
- Do not put an MPAN in a `DomainError` message. `problem()` copies the message into the
  response `title`, so it leaves the building. `findSite` does exactly that with
  `NotFound(site ${siteId})` - that is a known finding, not a pattern to copy.
- Never truncate an MPAN into a short id or cache key - the trailing digits are the
  distributor and meter portion and collide across regions.
- GET handlers read `siteId` from the request body, not the query string, on purpose - `Req`
  has no `query` field at all. It keeps MPANs out of request lines, proxy logs and history.
- `describeSite` folds the MPAN into its `label`; treat that string as an MPAN, not a name.

## Logs and analytics

- `log()` redacts by exact top-level key name - `mpan`, `meterSerial`, `customerRef` - and
  does not recurse. It is a backstop, not a boundary: `{ site: { mpan } }` writes the MPAN in
  full, and so does `{ siteId }`, the name the MPAN goes by here and the one `REDACT` omits.
- Widening `REDACT` is not the fix: it only narrows what a line may say, and the useful lines
  key on `siteId`. Do not pass the value. `rollupSite` logs `{ siteId, wh }` for one site
  today - that is the open finding, and it breaks the next rule too.
- Log the event and the shape, never the subject: counts, durations, row totals, error
  codes. Energy is loggable only above site level; one site's `wh` for one day is a household.
- Never log a request or response body: a billing body carries supplier `rate` values in its
  `tariffs` array as well as the MPAN, and we hold no agreement to keep supplier rates in the
  log store. Responses are no safer - `getSummary` echoes `siteId` straight back.
- Analytics gets rollups keyed by an export-time surrogate, never by `siteId`.
- Scar tissue: MPANs reached the log index through an unhandled error's `String(e)`, which
  bypasses `REDACT` entirely; retention there was 400 days, so it was a reportable breach.
  `normalizeUnit` still throws a plain `Error` holding its input - assume any message string
  built in the domain reaches the log store verbatim.

## Retention

| Data | Kept | Then |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Raw half-hourly readings | 24 months | deleted |
| Rollups and billing charges | 7 years | kept (tax) |
| Application logs | 30 days | deleted |
| Ingest dead-letter payloads | 7 days | deleted |

- 24 months is the settlement dispute window plus a quarter; longer needs a lawful basis.
- Rollups past 24 months must not be reversible to a half-hour; a finer grain needs an ADR.
- `readings` records only `bucket`, never an arrival time, so the quarterly delete keys on
  reading time. A late backfill is part-expired the day it lands; note that in the runbook.
- There is no purge job. Deletion is a manual quarterly runbook and our largest standing
  audit finding - never assume expired data is gone, and re-run it after any restore.

## Subject access and erasure

- SARs arrive through Legal on a 30-day clock. Engineering extracts, it does not set scope.
- Export is every `readings` row, the `sites` row, that site's `tariffs` rows and derived
  charges, converted with `toKwh` (ADR 001). A subject gets kWh, never raw `wh`.
- Erasure is usually refused in part: settlement data has a statutory basis. Restrict.
- The MPAN goes in the SAR register, not in a ticket title, branch name or code comment.

## DCC and third parties

- The DCC is a source, not a sink. We send nothing back but feed acknowledgements; any new
  outbound flow needs a DPIA before code is written.
- Never forward a raw provider payload into a ticket or vendor thread - reproduce with a
  synthetic 13-digit MPAN that passes `validateSiteId` and maps to no real distributor.
- Log and metrics vendors are sub-processors: register them before issuing a credential.

## Encryption at rest

- Volumes, snapshots and backups are encrypted at the platform layer with customer-managed
  keys. Nothing in this repo implements it and nothing should.
- `DATABASE_URL` must carry `sslmode=verify-full`. `loadConfig` does not check this, which is
  how a staging URL without it went unnoticed for a month.
- No meter data in local files: no debugging CSVs, no `.sql` snapshots, no pasting into chat.
